an Pamidimarri", "text": "next one has 3 parts, two of them have the same proper noun in them, so pay attention", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T16:16:43Z", "username": "Akshay", "text": "Yeh all caps mein bolna hota hai", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T16:16:51Z", "username": "Akshay", "text": "Absolutely insulting, visa cancel karo iska", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T16:16:55Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "13.\tOn April 25, 1990, _Space Shuttle Discovery_ released X from its cargo bay into orbit. When X sent its first images back to Earth, they were blurry. The mirror at its heart, which had been ground with extraordinary precision at a cost of hundreds of millions of dollars, had been ground to the wrong specification. The error was 2.2 microns — less than one-fiftieth the width of a human hair. NASA spent the next three years on a mission to repair X. Since then, the repaired X has transformed our understanding of the cosmos. It has measured the age of the universe (13.8 billion years), confirmed the existence of supermassive black holes at the center of most galaxies, and discovered that the expansion of the universe is accelerating — a finding that led to the concept of Y, the mysterious driver of that acceleration, which earned the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2011. Perhaps most famously, in 1995 and again in 2004, X photographed a tiny patch of sky that appeared completely dark — and found it filled with thousands of previously unknown galaxies, each containing hundreds of billions of stars. This image, known as Z, is considered one of the most important photographs in the history of science. ID X (7 points), Y (6 points), and Z (7 points). X and Z have a proper noun in common.", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false,